Turkey's wind
Global giants relying on Turkey's future have struggled against those who broke the wind power plant auction that took place yesterday.
The world record was set in the tender made by the Ministry of Energy and Natural Resources with the model designed to ensure the transition to national energy by reducing foreign dependence.
The tender, which will reduce Turkey's energy costs, paused $1 billion in investment and nearly 4,000 jobs.
Domestic production wind turbines will be incorporated into the system by the beginning of 2019.
8 consortiums, including U.S., Chinese, German and Danish giants participated in the licensing tender for renewable resource areas (YEKA) wind power plant opened by the Ministry of Energy and Natural Resources.
Financial proposal envelopes were first opened in order to determine 5 bidders who made the lowest bid.
After the initial bid envelopes, the lowest bid was $4.19/kilovatsaat.
The open diminishing range was set at 0.01 cent.
As the tender began, Enercon decided not to participate in the definitive undertaking groups of joint ventures involving Gold Wind, GE, Senvion and Vestas.
In the open diminishing, Siemens, Nordex and Mingyang competed.
MingYang-First Construction and Siemens-Türkerler-Calyon remained in the last two.
MingYang-First Construction took a break 11 times.
Siemens-Türkerler-Kalyon Energy consortium did not take a break.
Minister of Energy and Natural Resources Berat Albayrak: Record day for Turkey.
The vulnerabilization lasted 30 laps.
The strong competition between the two consortiums was the Siemens-Türkerler-Kalyon co-organization group, which gave the lowest bid with 3.48 dollars/pool.
The consortium offered $3.49 per cent/kilovat hours in the tender, while subsecretary Alparslan Bayraktar's presidency drew his offer to 3.48 at the request of the tender commission.
Turkey achieved a world record with the results of the two-a-hour YEKA tender.
$3.48/cent per kilowatt per kilowatth offered at the tender has become the world's lowest figure in this area.
The previous record was $3.84/cent per kilowatt.
Minister of Energy and Natural Resources Berat Albayrak, who held a press conference following the completion of the Tender, stated that winning the competition was an important message in the region and to the world. "I think this result will make a significant contribution to Turkey-Germany relations in terms of 200 years of historical co-operation of Turkey and Germany."
We've got big targets.
The result today has demonstrated how strong and confident the economy and the energy market is taking steps towards the future.
Regardless of regional crises in the world, I was very proud to present with this auction that Turkey is one of the most relievably investable countries in the region and in the world.
"In this picture, which we will be proud of as 80 million all of Turkey, the biggest contributor is our President who carried Turkey to this point within its 15-year power and leadership," he said.
Stating that they have started a new era with the tender where there is clear, transparent and comparative competition. "One of the most most important steps of this has been realized at a world-rencasing price by a world-rencasing price by falling to 3.48 dollars/agency with a domestic contribution share in the wind until yesterday.
That's what I'm so proud of for my people, on behalf of my country.
Minister Albayrak pointed out that they aimed to commission the first domestic production panels in solar energy before the end of 2018 and said, "I hope we will incorporate our domestic production wind turbines into the system by the beginning of 2019."
Turbins will be established in 5 designated zones.
The company that has won the competition will put into service a installed power of at least a thousand megawatts in each of these 5 regions with the condition of establishing a wind power of at least 50 megawatts.
Siemens-Turkerler-Kalyon group will invest more than 1 billion dollars.
With domestic wind power plants entering operation, a minimum of 3 billion kilowatt hours of electrical energy will be produced every year, and about 1.1 million homes' annual electricity needs will be covered by the wind.
Thanks to the wind facilities to be established on an average of 1.5 million tons of CO2/annual emission reduction will also be ensured.
The company that won the competition will set up a factory with more than $100m investment.
An average production of 300 to 450 domestic wind turbines will be carried out in the factory with a power of at least 2.3 megawatts.
Consortium will do R&D work for 10 years in at least three out of wing, generator design, material technologies and manufacturing techniques, software and innovative gearbox.
A $5 million budget is allocated for R&D studies every year, with 50 technical personnel, 80 per cent consisting of domestic engineers.
It'll be set up 21 months after the factory contract is signed.
The licensing period of the project is 30 years.
Kazım Türker, Chairman of the Board of Directors of Türkerler İnşaat, who took part in the consortium that won the tender, stated that domestic production will be around 64 percent in the project and 35 percent of the equipment will come from abroad and said, "We plan to finance the equipment from foreign sources and the rest from domestic banks."
Türker said the tender reflects confidence in the Turkish economy.
Kalyon Holding Energy Group President Murtaza Ata said, "It was a tender that would reduce Turkey's foreign dependency by a thousand megawatts."
"We're happy with the outcome," he said.
SIemens Gamesa Turkey Senior Manager Hakan Yıldırım also stated that they have been operating in Turkey for 160 years and said, "We will do everything we can to reduce the electric unit cost."
Wind turbines to be produced in the factory will be at least 65 per cent.
Of the 8,000 industrial parts found in the wind turbine, 65 per cent will be domestic (at least 5,200 parts).
