8-month return in Borsa Istanbul withstands 50 per cent
The BIST 100 index continued its ascension trend, which began in December 2016, and carried its ascension to the 8th month at the top.
The index thus peaked 8 months in a row for the first time since July 2007, earning 45.3 per cent during this period.
The BIST 100 index, which has surpassed the world's major stock markets, including Germany, the United States, the United Kingdom and Japan, with its performance since the beginning of the year, did not leave the lead on a pedestrian-based basis in July.
With an increase of 7.06 per cent in July, the BIST 100 index in the United States was followed by the Dow Jones index in the United States with an increase of 2.54 per cent and the FTSE 100 index at 0.81 per cent.
With the DAX index in Almaya, the Nikkei index in Japan ended July with depreciation.
As of January-July, it has been seen that the BIST 100 index has a higher performance from both the major world stock exchanges and the indices of the developing country's stock exchanges.
The BIST 100 index, which gained 37.6 per cent compared to the 2016 closing this year, the Dow Jones index with an increase of 10.76 per cent, the DAX index with a value gain of 5.55 per cent, Nikkei with an increase of 4.55 per cent and the FTSE 100 with an increase of 3.21 per cent.
The MSCI EM index, animator for the developing country's stock markets, amounted to 23 per cent.
Looking at the historical process of the BIST 100 index, it has been seen to show similar performances in the past.
The BIST 100 index increased by 38.3 per cent during this period, which last completed 8 months in a row between December 2006 and July 2007.
In addition, the BIST 100 index completed 8 months between August 1996 and March 1997 in 9 months between May 1997 and January 1998.
Meksa Investment Research Director Eren Can Ümüt told AA correspondent that the BIST 100 index continued to rise for 8 months, noting that the rise has exceeded 37 per cent since the beginning of the year.
ÜMut noted that MSCI evolving markets (EM) index has risen by 23 per cent and there is positive decomposition even when we look at these odds.
The flow of funds to developing countries has been continuing since the beginning of the year.
Participants, in search of high-receivables, prefer countries that give higher interest rates.
Domestic markets are positively affected by this situation.
Of course, on a global scale, the appetite to take risks is very high.
In this way, the stock indices, which have risky asset status, are in demand.
