New results of Sweden's asylum age evaluation test announced
The latest results of the evaluation tests of refugees subjected to a new method of medical age evaluation in Sweden have ruled that more than 80 per cent of respondents are older than the age of 18, but not everyone trusts the accuracy of these tests.
The National Forensic Medicine and Drug Administration of Sweden (Réttsmedicinalverket) started conducting tests earlier this year.
The tests were designed to make age evaluation more accurate in the asylum process after the Swedish Immigration Agency (Migrationsverket) criticized their failures in evaluating the right age of some refugees, who claimed they were underestimated.
To date, Migrationsverket has tested 6880 cases, and the Forensic Medical and Drug Administration has announced the results of a total of 2481 from the mid-March to July 31, 2017.
It was ruled that 80 per cent ($2002) were 18 or higher, and in 25 cases the Forensics and Drug Administration specifically ruled that "probably 18 and above."
In 432 cases, the individual was "probably below 18."
The tests were carried out only in cases where one's age was suspicious.
According to Johan Göransson of the Forensics and Drug Administration, the results are consistent in the past months.
"We don't see a big difference in statistics according to the past months, the figures are quite consistent," he told The Local.
The high rate of cases that have been ruled over the age of 18 does not necessarily mean that refugees are lying about their age.
In May, the apartment unveiled the results of the 581 tests and said there were 442 situations where "the viewers were 18 years of age or were shown to be older."
By examining the results of 581 cases announced by Svenska Dagbladet newspaper, 243 cases have clearly declared that they are 18 years old this year.
The medical age evaluation method, which includes x-raying the 20-year-old teeth and conduct an MRI screening of the knee joints and then analyzing them to dentists and radiologists, are criticized in some circles.
A skeptet is Claude Marcus, an endocrinologian of the Karolinska Institute, evaluating the biological maturity of the people who have studies on whether it is premature than usual or late.
"I have reasons to think about it, and I don't think it shouldn't be the measurement of the species used.
It's very skeptical to decimal deceiving like this.
If we can't say for sure, we can't pretend it is."
However, Göransson from the Forensic Medicine and Drug Administration advocactated the accuracy of the tests: "In general, you may say the risk of misjudgizing a child as an adult is misjudged.
"The biggest risk is just under the age of 18, in this case it's around 10 per cent."
