Facebook increases story verification power to fight counterfeit news
Facebook will send more potential counterfeit articles to third-party narrative validators and will show their findings below the original sharing; the world's largest online social network on Thursday described it trying to fight the so-called falsified news.
In a statement made on the company's website, he said they would use updated machine learning to identify possible fraudulators and send them to narrative validators, which would potentially demonstrate story verification results under the original article.
Facebook has been criticized as one of the main deployment postings of news, known as a fake news that many think has influenced the 2016 US presidential election.
The problem became a major political stance in Europe, when French constituency was flooded prior to the presidential election in May and Germany fails to quickly remove hate-filled social media networks before the presidential election.
On Thursday, Facebook said in a separate statement in German that a test of the new story verification feature is being launched in the United States, France, the Netherlands and Germany.
"Including to see which stories are being discussed by the third-party narrative confirmers, people want more resources to read and share information-based queues."
He added that Facebook will continue to test "responsibly article" feature and will work on other changes in the news stream to mitigate counterfeit news.
