Interpol's live-torage list 6 Europeans
The German newspaper Die Welt reported that Interpol (International Police Service) sent a list of 173 people who had the potential to carry out suicide bombings on behalf of Daesh last May to law enforcement agencies in European countries.
It was reported that 132 people on the list were from Iraq, North Africa, Gulf countries, Tajikistan and Bangladesh.
The six people on the list were said to be European citizens.
They come from Germany, Belgium, France, the Netherlands and Bosnia.
It has been reported to be a 27-year-year-old German citizen from Solingen, North Rhine-Westphalia.
It was stated that he went first to Egypt in the autumn of 2012, then Turkey, and finally to Syria.
Daesh announced on social media accounts in mid-July that this person had died in Raqqa.
It has been noted that the German Police Department did not answer the newspaper's questions on the issue.
It has been suggested that the names on Interpol's list have been recovered during operations aimed at Daesh in Mosul.
According to Die Welt, U.S. S.E. intelligence services have reviewed the list and sent it to Interpol.
Interpol also noted that he transferred the list to police agencies in Europe in May.
Die Welt reported that Daesh may attempt to send some of the names on this list to Europe to organise suicide-rism attacks.
